  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to leverage multi-scale basis functions to capture both long-term structure and short-term details in the optimal solution. This approach attempts to break free from the local optima that may result from single-resolution optimization. We use a hierarchical structure: starting with a coarse grid of few points and gradually increasing resolution, while optimizing a sparse representation that adapts across scales. The method combines random initialization with gradient-based optimization and maintains a memory of past optima to facilitate exploration.\n\nKey rationale:\n- Single-resolution optimization may be trapped by local optima\n- Multi-scale representation allows capturing both large and small features\n- Hierarchical optimization reduces the risk of premature convergence\n- The method is less sensitive to hyperparameter tuning and more robust across different resolutions\n- Memory of past optima provides a natural restart mechanism\n\nLimitations:\n- Requires careful handling of basis function transitions\n- Computation time scales with resolution growth\n- Hyperparameter tuning may still be needed\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Representation: h(x) approximated as a linear combination of orthogonal basis functions with adaptive sparsity\n- Variable set: h = \u2211_j c_j \u03c6_j(x), where \u03c6_j are orthogonal basis functions and c_j are coefficients\n- Objective: C5 = max_k \u222b_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n- Constraints: \u222b_0^2 h(x) dx = 1 and 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 \u2200x\n- Basis functions: Hierarchical wavelets/del\u00e0a function basis, chosen for their ability to represent discontinuities and smoothness variations\n- Smoothness parameter: \u03b3 controls the trade-off between basis function scale and coefficient sparsity\n- Projection method: Iterative clipping and adjustment to maintain [0,1] bounds while preserving \u222b h(x) dx = 1\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   Initial preparation:\n      - Set default n_start = 30 if parent not available else parent's n\n      - Set deadline tracking within the function\n      - Initialize log of objectives and best candidates\n\n   Core loop:\n      Phase 1: Coarsening stage\n        - Start with few samples (n=4, alpha=50)\n        - Use simple basis functions (piecewise constant)\n        - Random search initialization (45% budget)\n        - Keep best candidate (with history logging)\n\n      Phase 2: Multi-scale progression\n        - Double resolution iteratively (55% budget)\n        - For each scale (n=4,8,16,32,... up to budget)\n        - Update basis functions by adding appropriate scales\n        - Perform local optimization at fixed points\n        - Project to maintain integral and bounds\n        - Update budget and switch if maxed\n\n      Phase 3: Final optimization for the best candidate\n        - Complete remaining budget on the best candidate\n        - Apply gradient descent with smoothing if available\n        - Incorporate memory to explore new regions\n\n   Evaluation:\n      - Compute C5 as max of cross-correlation integrals\n      - Ensure integral is 1 and all values in [0,1]\n      - Compare C5 values with interpolation for different n\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n   Default progression:\n      - Start n = 30 samples\n      - Basis scale gamma = 0.2 initially\n      - Add sparsity factor (0.1) each progression\n      - Optimization method: SLSQP for few variables (low sample numbers), gradient descent with smoothing for medium samples\n\n   Key variations:\n      - Coarsening ratio (2x or 5x)\n        default=2 (less aggressive)\n      - Basis function type (wavelets vs NURBS)\n        default=wavelets (better for discontinuities)\n      - Sparsity control (0.05 to 0.3)\n        default=0.15\n      - Scales optimization: \n          - Try multiple bases in low-budg phase\n          - Memory-based restart if stuck\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   Time estimates:\n      - Coarsening: 200 sec for n=4 (4%, budget=200)\n      - Doubling: each n\u00d72 takes linearly growing time\n      - Total max iterations: ~5-8 doublings (for 1100 sec)\n\n   Schedule:\n      - Run coarsening phase until budget \u2265 200\n      - Then double resolution every 10 steps (increasing budget)\n      - Check progress every 100 sec\n      - If budget < 100 sec: skip to final optimization\n\n   Early termination:\n      - If no improvement in 5 steps, restart from memory\n      - If computation exceeds time, gracefully reduce resolution\n      - Use checkpointing to restore progress\n\n   Low-budget fallback:\n      - If budget < 300 sec, default to the parent solution\n      - Store all checkpoints to allow budget reuse\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   Final checks:\n      - Verify integral is exactly 1\n      - Clip values to [0,1] and recompute if needed\n      - Compute C5 with actual integrals and return log details\n\n   Output requirements:\n      - h_values: Numpy array of step values\n      - c5_bound: Numerically stable max overlap value\n      - n_points: Number of samples used\n      - Internal logging: objective logs for last 10 candidates\n\n   Memory lifecycle:\n      - Maintain best solution at each resolution\n      - Store top K candidates for each grid\n      - Reuse history for restarts",
